// Cross-process SHM delivery, LARGE payload (~4 KB) — Hale READER.
//
// Subscribes to the ~4 KB flat-struct shm ring. For EACH record it does
// real work on the WHOLE payload — sums all 512 Int fields into a
// running checksum — so the per-record read genuinely touches all
// 4096 bytes and the work can't be optimized away. Times from the
// first record to the last (counts to n), prints iters / elapsed_ns /
// checksum so correctness is assertable.
//
// Hale's shm_ring `where zero_copy` path dispatches the handler with a
// pointer straight into the shared-memory slot (verified in
// lotus_shm_ring.c: shm_ring_reader_thread -> read_slot returns
// ring->slots_base + idx*slot_size, no memcpy / deserialize), so the
// 512 field reads below happen IN PLACE — no per-record 4096-byte
// copy into the subscriber arena. (This holds only because every field
// is a flat scalar; a fixed-array field would be an out-of-line pointer
// and dangle cross-process — see the producer's layout note.)
